# models/ stays ignored WHOLESALE — it holds multi-GB weights (11 Sep 2026:
# models/cortex-l1/qwen2.5-3b-instruct.Q4_K_M.gguf is 1.79 GB) and this is a
# PUBLIC repo. Deliberately NOT loosened to `models/*` with negations: that
# requires un-ignoring the parent, and one careless `git add` away from a 2 GB
# blob in a history that cannot forget it. The two small files that DO belong in
# git — models/cortex-l1/Modelfile and models/cortex-l1/L1_RESULT.json — were
# force-added once (`git add -f`) and stay tracked from then on, so the rule
# below never has to make an exception for them.

models/
memory/blackbox.jsonl

# ─── ITEM 81 — RUNTIME STATE IS NOT CODE (11 September 2026) ────────────────
#
# WHY THIS BLOCK EXISTS, and it is not tidiness. Until today the machine's own
# memory was tracked in git and rewritten every night. Three things followed
# from that, all measured rather than feared:
#
#  1. A CREDENTIAL REACHED A COMMIT. A push of 21 commits to the PUBLIC repo was
#     stopped on 11 Sep because a live-shaped Google API key sat in nine log
#     lines under memory/ — a Gemini error had echoed the request URL, which
#     carries ?key=... . core/redact.py now scrubs the write path, but the
#     reason the leak could reach a public diff at all is this: nightly logs
#     were tracked. A log is not source.
#  2. BRANCHES BECAME UNSWITCHABLE. Measured for ITEM 77: 85 files were
#     simultaneously uncommitted-modified AND different between the two
#     branches — cortex_memory/state.json, archive/merkle_root.txt, 77
#     data/initiatives/*.json, the snapshots. A checkout would have rolled back
#     a night of accumulated state, so "which branch runs the night" could not
#     be a cheap decision.
#  3. A RESET ALREADY DESTROYED SOME. docs/QUEUE.md RESET_DAMAGE: the 28 Aug
#     `git reset --hard` replaced snapshots/master/global_indicators_latest.json
#     with an older committed copy, and two tests still fail because of it.
#
# WHAT STAYS TRACKED, deliberately: every .py and .md under memory/ (they are
# code and documentation, not state), memory/existence_ledger.jsonl and
# memory/first_bet/ (dated, append-only audit records, both verified free of
# credential-shaped strings before this line was written), and everything under
# config/, which is a decision a human made rather than a number a night
# produced.
#
# The rule to apply when adding to this list: if a cycle rewrites it, it is
# state; if a human edits it, it is code.
memory/*.json
memory/*.jsonl
memory/*.npz
memory/web_intelligence/
memory/body_sensorium/
memory/composer_state/
memory/steps/
memory/phase_debriefs/
!memory/existence_ledger.jsonl
!memory/first_bet/
# The gate's audit trail. Emil, 11 Sep: it stays tracked, and the condition was
# a scrubber check first — run, and it is clean (4 rows, 3,027 chars, no
# credential pattern, byte-identical after redact()). Kept for the same reason as
# the ledgers: every row is a verdict core/quote_gate.py reached against a live
# page, it is what training/verified_corpus.py builds from, and a claim about the
# sensor's invention rate has to be checkable later. Its other half,
# memory/card_refusals.jsonl, is NOT tracked — say so and it can be, on the same
# condition.
!memory/verified_observations.jsonl
snapshots/master/*_latest.json

# ── L1 / L1b LESSON CORPORA (12 Sep 2026) — THE GENERATOR IS COMMITTED, ITS OUTPUT IS NOT.
# 15.7 MB + 2.1 MB of generated lessons:
#   training/lora_l1_numbers.py   -> l1_numbers_train.jsonl, l1_numbers_holdout.jsonl
#   training/lora_l1b_numbers.py  -> l1b_train.jsonl, l1b_exam.jsonl
# The MANIFESTS are committed and carry the seeds (l1b: seed_train 1211, seed_exam 9121)
# and the layout/group counts, so a regenerated corpus can be checked against the
# manifest it claims to be. Same convention as memory/: the code is committed, the data
# it generates is not.
#
# BUT THE SEED IS NOT THE WHOLE INPUT, and saying otherwise would be a false promise.
# L1b's long wrapper is built from memory/l1_real_prompt_blocks.json, whose `body` and
# `self_state` blocks are read off THIS MACHINE at dump time ("RAM 98.2% used",
# "OPEN_PROPOSALS: 25 (20 overdue)") and then jittered deterministically from the seed.
# So l1b_train.jsonl is byte-for-byte reproducible from (seed + that blocks file), and
# NOT from the seed alone: a later --dump-prompt writes different numbers. The blocks
# file is untracked runtime state under memory/ (ITEM 81/84), so today the manifest's
# "wrapper: REAL brain.think blocks" is a claim about a file git does not hold. If that
# corpus ever needs to be re-derivable by someone else, the blocks file has to be
# committed alongside the manifest — it is 4.7 KB and scans clean.
#
# EXPLICITLY LISTED, NO PATTERN. `training/*.jsonl` would also cover
# training/verified_corpus.jsonl, which IS tracked — and a rule that silently
# disagrees with the state of git is worse than a missing rule (see the two blocks
# above that learned this the same way).
training/l1_numbers_train.jsonl
training/l1_numbers_holdout.jsonl
training/l1b_train.jsonl
training/l1b_exam.jsonl
